<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><h2><b>How to Build a Brick Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Brick pavers bring a warmth and traditional character that concrete pavers don’t quite replicate, which is exactly why so many homeowners gravitate toward brick for a front entry or garden path. The construction process shares a lot of DNA with any paver walkway project, base prep, leveling, laying, jointing, but brick itself has a few quirks worth understanding before you start, from how it handles moisture to how it behaves differently than concrete once it’s cut.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, we build brick paver walkways across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, and brick remains one of the most requested materials for homeowners going for a classic, timeless look. This guide walks through the full process of building a brick walkway, how brick compares to concrete pavers, and what to know if you’re considering reclaimed or antique brick for the project.</p>
<h2><b>Brick Pavers Versus Concrete Pavers</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Factor</b></td><td><b>Brick Pavers</b></td><td><b>Concrete Pavers</b></td></tr><tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Classic, warm, traditional</td><td>Wide range, from traditional to modern</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Generally higher per square foot</td><td>Generally lower per square foot</td></tr><tr><td>Durability</td><td>Very durable, can chip at edges over time</td><td>Very durable, resists chipping well</td></tr><tr><td>Moisture absorption</td><td>Higher, can affect freeze-thaw performance</td><td>Lower, more freeze-thaw resistant in most products</td></tr><tr><td>Cutting</td><td>Can crack unpredictably along the cut</td><td>Cuts more predictably with the right blade</td></tr><tr><td>Color consistency</td><td>Can vary noticeably between batches</td><td>Generally more consistent between batches</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Brick’s biggest practical difference from concrete is moisture absorption. Brick is naturally more porous, which means it can hold more water than a dense concrete paver, and that extra moisture matters more in a climate with regular freeze and thaw cycles, since absorbed water expanding as it freezes is one of the main causes of surface flaking or spalling on brick over time. This isn’t a reason to avoid brick, it’s just a reason to choose the right brick product and sealing approach for the climate, which we’ll get into further down.</p>
<h2><b>Choosing the Right Brick for a Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Clay Brick Pavers</b></h3>
<p>Standard clay brick pavers, fired at high temperatures specifically for exterior paving use, are denser and less porous than traditional building brick, making them a much better choice for a walkway than repurposed wall brick, which isn’t rated for the same freeze-thaw exposure or foot traffic.</p>
<h3><b>Reclaimed or Antique Brick</b></h3>
<p>Reclaimed brick brings a genuinely unique, aged character that new brick simply can’t replicate, and it’s a popular choice for homeowners going for a more historic or rustic look. That said, reclaimed brick comes with some real considerations. Older brick, especially building brick reclaimed from demolished structures, wasn’t always made for ground-level foot traffic and can be more porous and prone to freeze damage than brick manufactured specifically for paving. If you’re set on a reclaimed look, it’s worth having each batch inspected for density and condition, and consider sealing more proactively than you would with new paving brick.</p>
<h3><b>Consistent Sourcing for Larger Projects</b></h3>
<p>Brick color and size can vary meaningfully between manufacturing batches, more so than with concrete pavers. For a walkway of any real length, it’s worth purchasing all the brick from a single batch where possible, since mixing batches can result in a visibly patchy color pattern once the walkway is finished.</p>
<h2><b>What You’ll Need</b></h2>
<p><b>Materials</b></p>
<ul><li>Brick pavers rated for exterior paving use, plus 10 to 15 percent extra for cuts and breakage</li><li>Crushed gravel or road base, 4 to 6 inches deep</li><li>Landscape fabric</li><li>Coarse bedding sand</li><li>Polymeric sand for joints</li><li>Edge restraints and spikes</li></ul>
<p><b>Tools</b></p>
<ul><li>Shovel and spade</li><li>A<a href="/blog/choose-the-right-plate-compactor-size-for-a-patio-or-driveway/"> properly sized plate compactor</a></li><li>Rubber mallet</li><li>String line and stakes</li><li>4 foot level</li><li>Wet saw with a masonry blade, brick tends to chip with a dry cut angle grinder more than concrete does</li><li>Push broom</li></ul>

