<h2><b>Step by Step: Building a Brick Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<h3><b>Step 1: Plan Your Pattern</b></h3>
<p>Brick walkways lean naturally toward traditional patterns like herringbone, basketweave, and running bond, all of which suit brick’s smaller, uniform format well. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-lay-a-paver-walkway/"> how to lay a paver walkway</a> covers these patterns in more depth, and the same technique and difficulty considerations apply directly to brick.</p>
<h3><b>Step 2: Excavate and Establish Level</b></h3>
<p>Mark your layout and excavate to a depth accounting for base, sand, and brick thickness combined, generally landing around 6 to 8 inches total, slightly less than a thicker concrete paver product since brick tends to run thinner. Our guide on<a href="/blog/how-to-level-ground-for-a-paver-walkway/"> leveling ground for a paver walkway</a> covers the tools and techniques for this stage in detail.</p>
<h3><b>Step 3: Build and Compact the Base</b></h3>
<p>Add crushed gravel in 2 inch layers, compacting each one before adding the next. Base depth recommendations are the same as for any paver walkway, generally 4 to 6 inches depending on soil type, with clay-heavy soil across parts of the Denver area benefiting from the fuller 6 inch depth.</p>
<h3><b>Step 4: Add Landscape Fabric and Bedding Sand</b></h3>
<p>Roll out landscape fabric over the compacted base, then add and screed about 1 inch of coarse sand to create a level surface for the brick.</p>
<h3><b>Step 5: Lay the Brick Following Your Pattern</b></h3>
<p>Place brick pavers snugly against each other, tapping each into place with a rubber mallet. Brick’s smaller format means more individual pieces per square foot compared to standard concrete pavers, so patterns like herringbone take noticeably longer to lay than they would with larger format pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Step 6: Cut With a Wet Saw, Not a Dry Blade</b></h3>
<p>Brick is more prone to unpredictable cracking than concrete when cut with a dry angle grinder. A wet saw designed for masonry produces cleaner, more controlled cuts and significantly reduces the chance of a brick cracking in the wrong place partway through a cut.</p>
<h3><b>Step 7: Install Edge Restraints</b></h3>
<p>Secure edge restraints tightly against the outer edge of the brick field. This step matters just as much for brick as for any paver material, keeping the entire pattern locked in place against seasonal ground movement.</p>
<h3><b>Step 8: Fill Joints With Polymeric Sand</b></h3>
<p>Sweep polymeric sand into the joints, remove excess from the surface, and mist lightly to activate. Brick’s slightly more textured, porous surface can hold a bit more residual sand than smoother concrete pavers, so a careful final sweep matters for a clean finished look.</p>
<h3><b>Step 9: Compact and Consider Sealing</b></h3>
<p>Run the plate compactor over the finished walkway one final time. Given brick’s higher moisture absorption, sealing the finished walkway is worth stronger consideration here than it might be for concrete pavers, particularly in a climate with regular freeze and thaw cycles.</p>
<h2><b>Should You Seal a Brick Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<p>Sealing isn’t strictly required, but it offers real benefits for brick specifically, given its natural porosity. A quality paver sealer reduces water absorption, which in turn reduces the risk of surface flaking during freeze and thaw cycles, and it also helps brick resist staining from dirt, oil, and de-icing salt residue over time. Reapplying sealer every two to three years is a reasonable maintenance rhythm for a brick walkway in this climate.</p>
<h2><b>Common Mistakes Specific to Brick Walkways</b></h2>
<ul><li>Using building brick instead of brick specifically rated for exterior paving</li><li>Cutting brick with a dry blade instead of a wet saw, leading to unpredictable cracking</li><li>Mixing brick from different manufacturing batches, resulting in a patchy color pattern</li><li>Skipping sealing in a climate with regular freeze and thaw cycles, increasing the risk of surface flaking</li><li>Using reclaimed brick without checking its density and suitability for ground-level foot traffic</li></ul>
<h2><b>Brick Walkway Cost Considerations</b></h2>
<p>Brick pavers generally cost more per square foot than standard concrete pavers, both in material price and in labor, since brick’s smaller format means more individual pieces to lay and cut for the same square footage. Reclaimed or antique brick often costs even more, both for the material itself and for the additional care needed in sourcing and inspecting it. Our<a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-paver-walkway-cost/"> paver walkway cost guide</a> covers general pricing that applies to paver walkways broadly, with brick typically landing toward the higher end of that range.</p>
<h2><b>Why We Lean Toward Recommending a Professional for Brick</b></h2>
<p>Brick’s tendency to crack unpredictably when cut incorrectly, its higher moisture sensitivity, and the batch consistency issues that come up when sourcing material all add a bit more complexity than a standard concrete paver project. None of this makes brick a bad DIY choice for a motivated, patient homeowner, but the margin for a costly mistake, cracked brick, a patchy color mismatch, or moisture damage from skipping sealer, is a bit higher than with concrete. We generally lean toward recommending professional installation for brick specifically, more so than for a comparable concrete paver project.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of building brick and concrete paver walkways across the Denver area, our team has a good sense of how brick behaves differently at each stage of construction, from sourcing to cutting to long-term sealing needs. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see the range of material and pattern work we’ve built for homeowners going for a classic brick look.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Is brick more expensive than concrete pavers for a walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Generally yes, both in material cost and labor, since brick’s smaller format requires more individual pieces and cutting for the same square footage compared to standard concrete pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Can I use reclaimed brick for a walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, but it’s worth having reclaimed brick inspected for density and condition first, since older building brick wasn’t always made for ground-level foot traffic or the same freeze-thaw exposure as brick manufactured for paving.</p>
<h3><b>Do I need to seal a brick paver walkway?</b></h3>
<p>It’s not strictly required, but it’s recommended given brick’s natural porosity, particularly in a climate with regular freeze and thaw cycles where absorbed moisture can lead to surface flaking.</p>
<h3><b>What’s the best way to cut brick pavers?</b></h3>
<p>A wet saw designed for masonry produces cleaner, more predictable cuts than a dry angle grinder, which can cause brick to crack unpredictably along the cut line.</p>
<h3><b>Does brick need a different base than concrete pavers?</b></h3>
<p>No, base depth recommendations are generally the same, 4 to 6 inches of compacted gravel depending on soil type, regardless of whether you’re using brick or concrete pavers.</p>
<h3><b>Why does my brick walkway look patchy in color?</b></h3>
<p>This is often caused by mixing brick from different manufacturing batches. Purchasing all brick for a project from a single batch helps avoid this issue.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build a Brick Walkway That Lasts</b></h2>
<p>Brick brings a classic, warm character to a walkway, but it comes with a few material-specific considerations worth getting right from the start.</p>
